      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To determine how many different positive four-digit integers can be formed using the digits 2, 2, 9, and 9, we need to consider the unique arrangements of these digits. Since the digits 2 and 2, and 9 and 9 are identical, we need to count the distinct permutations of these digits while accounting for the repetition.\n\nThe formula for the number of distinct permutations of a multiset is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n!}{n_1! \\cdot n_2! \\cdot \\ldots \\cdot n_k!}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( n \\) is the total number of elements to arrange, and \\( n_1, n_2, \\ldots, n_k \\) are the frequencies of the distinct objects.\n\nIn this problem, we have:\n- \\( n = 4 \\) (total digits),\n- \\( n_1 = 2 \\) (frequency of the digit 2),\n- \\( n_2 = 2 \\) (frequency of the digit 9).\n\nPlugging these values into the formula, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{4!}{2! \\cdot 2!}\n\\]\n\nFirst, we calculate the factorials:\n\\[\n4! = 4 \\times 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 24\n\\]\n\\[\n2! = 2 \\times 1 = 2\n\\]\n\nNow, substitute these values back into the formula:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{24}{2 \\cdot 2} = \\frac{24}{4} = 6\n\\]\n\nThus, the number of different positive four-digit integers that can be formed using the digits 2, 2, 9, and 9 is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]",
